== The Spirit of Jesus Christ

VIII. Truly gracious affections differ from those that are false and delusive in that they tend #emph[to,] and are attended #emph[with,] the lamb-like, dove-like Spirit and disposition of Jesus Christ. In other words, they naturally beget and promote such a spirit of love, meekness, quietness, forgiveness and mercy as appeared in Christ.

The evidence for this in the Scripture is very abundant. If we judge the nature of Christianity and the proper spirit of the gospel by the word of God, this spirit is what may, above all others, be called #emph[the Christian spirit.] It may be regarded as the true and distinguishing disposition of the hearts of Christians as Christians. When some of Christ’s disciples, through thoughtlessness and weakness, said something that did not agree with such a spirit, Christ told them that they did not know what kind of spirit they were of (Luke 9:55). This implies that the spirit I am speaking of is the proper spirit of his religion and kingdom. All who are truly godly and real disciples of Christ have this spirit in them. And not only that: they #emph[are of] this spirit. It is the spirit by which they are so possessed and governed that it is their true and proper character. This is evident from what the wise man says in Prov 17:27 (plainly having in view such a spirit as this): “A man of understanding is of a calm spirit.” It is evident too from the particular description Christ gives in Matt 5 of the qualities and disposition of those who are truly blessed, who will obtain mercy, and who are God’s children and heirs: “Blessed are the meek, for they will inherit the earth. … Blessed are the merciful, for they will be shown mercy. … Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called sons of God.” And that this spirit is the special character of the elect of God is clear from Colossians 3:12, 13: “Therefore, as the elect of God, holy and beloved, clothe yourselves with hearts of compassion, kindness, humility, meekness, and patience. Bear with one another and forgive” one another. The apostle also speaks of that spirit and disposition which he presents as the most excellent and essential thing in Christianity, #emph[that] without which none are true Christians, and without which the most glorious profession and gifts are nothing. He calls this spirit love, and he describes it like this (1 Cor 13:4, 5): “Love is patient, love is kind. It does not envy, it does not boast, it is not proud. It is not rude, it is not self-seeking, it is not easily angered, it keeps no account of wrongs.” And in Gal 5, where the same apostle sets out on purpose the distinguishing marks and fruits of true Christian grace, he dwells chiefly on the things that belong to such a disposition and spirit as I am speaking of, verses 22, 23: “The fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, meekness, and self-control.” So does the apostle #emph[James] in describing true grace, or “the wisdom from above.” He does it with the declared aim that others who are of a contrary spirit may not deceive themselves and lie against the truth by professing to be Christians when they are not, Jas 3:14–17: “But if you#super[+] harbor bitter jealousy and strife in your#super[+] hearts, do not boast in it or lie against the truth. Such wisdom does not come from above, but is earthly, unspiritual, demonic. For where jealousy and strife exist, there will be disorder and every evil practice. But the wisdom from above is first of all pure, then peace-loving, gentle, accommodating, full of mercy and good fruit.”

Everything that belongs to holiness of heart does indeed belong to the nature of true Christianity and the character of Christians. But a spirit of holiness, as it appears in some particular graces, may more especially be called the Christian spirit or disposition. There are some lovely qualities and virtues that agree more especially with the nature of the gospel order and the Christian profession. One reason is that there is a special agreement between them and those divine attributes which God has more remarkably shown and glorified in the work of redemption by Jesus Christ, the grand subject of the Christian revelation. There is also a special agreement between them and the virtues Jesus Christ so wonderfully exercised toward us in that work, and the blessed example he has set us in it. Another reason is that they agree especially with the particular drift and design of the work of redemption, and with the benefits we receive by it, and the relation it brings us into, to God and to one another. These virtues are such as humility, meekness, love, forgiveness and mercy. These things, therefore, belong especially to the character of Christians as such.

These things are spoken of as being especially the character of Jesus Christ himself, the great head of the Christian church. They are spoken of so in the prophecies of the Old Testament, as in the one cited in Matt 21:5: “Say to the Daughter of Zion, ‘See, your King comes to you, meek and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey.’ ” Christ himself speaks of them so in Matt 11:29: “Learn from Me; for I am meek and humble in heart.” The same appears from the name by which Christ is so often called in Scripture, namely THE LAMB. And as these things are especially the character of Christ, so they are also especially the character of Christians. Christians are Christlike. None deserve the name of Christians who are not so in their prevailing character. “The new man … is being renewed … in the image of its Creator” (Col 3:10). All true Christians, “beholding as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, are being transformed into the same image,” by his Spirit (2 Cor 3:18). The elect are all predestined “to be conformed to the image of His Son, so that He would be the firstborn among many brothers” (Rom 8:29). “Just as we have borne the image of the earthly man, so also must we bear the image of the heavenly man. … As was the earthly man, so also are those who are of the earth; and as is the heavenly man, so also are those who are of heaven” (1 Cor 15:47, 48, 49). Christ is full of grace, and Christians all “received of his fullness, and grace for grace.” That is, there is grace in Christians answering to grace in Christ, a correspondence like that between the wax and the seal: there is character for character. Such kinds of graces, such a spirit and disposition, the same things that belong to Christ’s character, belong to theirs. His image consists especially in that disposition in which Christ’s character especially consists. Christians who shine by reflecting the light of the Sun of righteousness shine with the same sort of brightness, the same mild, sweet and pleasant beams. These lamps of the spiritual temple, which are kindled by fire from heaven, burn with the same sort of flame. The branch is of the same nature as the stock and root, has the same sap, and bears the same sort of fruit. The members have the same kind of life as the head. It would be strange if Christians were not of the same disposition and spirit that Christ is of, when they are his flesh and his bone, indeed are “one with Him in spirit” (1 Cor 6:17), and live in such a way that it is not they who live, but Christ who lives in them. A Christian spirit is Christ’s mark, which he sets on the souls of his people: his seal in their foreheads, bearing his image and inscription. Christians are the followers of Christ, and they are so as they obey that call of Christ in Matt 11:28, 29: “Come to Me … learn from Me; for I am meek and humble in heart.” They follow him as the Lamb, Rev 14:4: “They follow the Lamb wherever He goes.” True Christians are, as it were, clothed with the meek, quiet and loving disposition of Christ; for #emph[all who are in Christ have put on Christ.] And in this respect #emph[the church is clothed with the sun,] not only by being clothed with his imputed righteousness, but also by being adorned with his graces (Rom 13:14). Christ the great Shepherd is himself a Lamb, and believers are also lambs; the whole flock are lambs. John 21:15: “Feed My lambs.” Luke 10:3: “I am sending you#super[+] out like lambs among wolves.” The redemption of the church by Christ from the power of the devil was typified long ago by #emph[David]’s delivering the #emph[lamb] out of the mouth of the lion and the bear.

That such virtue as has been spoken of is the very nature of the Christian spirit, the spirit that works in Christ and in his members, and its distinguishing nature, is evident from this: #emph[the dove] is the very symbol or emblem God chose to represent it. Those things are the fittest emblems of other things which best represent #emph[that] which is most distinguishing in their nature. The Spirit that descended on Christ when he was anointed by the Father descended on him #emph[like a dove.] The dove is a well-known emblem of meekness, harmlessness, peace and love. But the same Spirit that descended on the head of the church descends to the members. “God sent the Spirit of His Son into” their “hearts” (Gal 4:6). “And if anyone does not have the Spirit of Christ, he does not belong to Christ” (Rom 8:9). There is only one Spirit for the whole mystical body, head and members (1 Cor 6:17; Eph 4:4). Christ breathes his own Spirit on his disciples (John 20:22). As Christ was anointed with the Holy Spirit descending on him like a dove, so Christians also “have an anointing from the Holy One” (1 John 2:20, 27). And they are anointed with the same oil. It is the same “fine oil on the head” that runs down “to the skirts of his robes,” and on both it is a spirit of peace and love. Ps 133:1, 2: “Behold, how good and pleasant it is when brothers live together in harmony! It is like fine oil on the head, running down on the beard, running down #emph[Aaron]’s beard to the skirts of his robes.” The oil on #emph[Aaron]’s garments had the same sweet and inimitable fragrance as that on his head, the smell of the same sweet spices. Christian affections and Christian behavior are only the flowing out of the fragrance of Christ’s sweet ointments. Because the church has a dove-like temper and disposition, it is said of her that she has doves’ eyes. Song 1:15: “How beautiful you are, my darling! Oh, how very beautiful! Your eyes are like doves.” And 4:1: “How beautiful you are, my darling—how very beautiful! Your eyes are like doves behind your veil.” The same is said of Christ in 5:12: “His eyes are like doves.” And the church is often compared to a dove in Scripture. Song 2:14: “O my dove in the clefts of the rock …” 5:2: “Open to me, … my darling, my dove.” And 6:9: “My dove, my perfect one, is unique.” Ps 68:13: “You#super[+] shall be like the wings of a dove covered with silver, and her feathers with shimmering gold.” And 74:19: “Do not deliver the soul of Your dove to beasts.” The dove that #emph[Noah] sent out of the ark, which could find no rest for the sole of her foot until she returned, was a type of a true saint.

Meekness is so much the character of the saints that #emph[the meek] and #emph[the godly] are used as synonymous terms in Scripture. So in Ps 37:10, 11 the wicked and the meek are set in opposition to one another, as the wicked and the godly: “Yet a little while, and the wicked will be no more … But the meek will inherit the land.” So too Ps 147:6: “The #smallcaps[Lord] sustains the meek, but casts the wicked to the ground.”

It is doubtless very much on this account that Christ represents all his disciples, all the heirs of heaven, as little children. Matt 19:14: “Let the little children come to Me, and do not hinder them! For the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these.” Matt 10:42: “And if anyone gives even a cup of cold water to one of these little ones because he is My disciple, truly I tell you#super[+], he will never lose his reward.” Matt 18:6: “But if anyone causes one of these little ones … to stumble,” and so on. Verse 10: “See that you#super[+] do not look down on any of these little ones.” Verse 14: “Your#super[+] Father in heaven is not willing that any of these little ones should perish.” John 13:33: “Little children, I am with you#super[+] only a little while longer.” Little children are innocent and harmless. They do not do a great deal of mischief in the world; men need not be afraid of them; they are not a dangerous sort of persons. Their anger does not last long. They do not store up injuries in deep resentment, harboring deep and rooted malice. So Christians are children in malice (1 Cor 14:20). Little children are not cunning and deceitful, but plain and simple. They are not skilled in the arts of pretense and deceit, and are strangers to crafty disguises. They are yielding and pliable, not willful and obstinate. They do not trust their own understanding, but rely on the instructions of their parents and others of greater understanding. Here, then, is a fit and lively emblem of the followers of the Lamb. Being like little children in this way is not only highly commendable, something Christians approve of and aim at, and which some of extraordinary progress attain. It is their universal character, and absolutely necessary for entering the kingdom of heaven, unless Christ was mistaken. Matt 18:3: “Truly I tell you#super[+], unless you#super[+] are converted and become like little children, you#super[+] will never enter the kingdom of heaven.” Mark 10:15: “Truly I tell you#super[+], anyone who does not receive the kingdom of God like a little child will never enter it.”

But here some may be ready to say: Is there no such thing as Christian fortitude and boldness for Christ, being good soldiers in the Christian warfare, and coming out boldly against the enemies of Christ and his people?

To this I answer that there doubtless is such a thing. The whole Christian life is compared to a warfare, and fitly so. And the most eminent Christians are the best soldiers, endowed with the greatest degrees of Christian fortitude. It is the duty of God’s people to be steadfast and vigorous in their opposition to the designs and ways of those who are trying to overthrow the kingdom of Christ and the cause of religion. But many persons seem to be quite mistaken about the nature of Christian fortitude. It is an exceedingly different thing from a brutal fierceness, or the boldness of beasts of prey. True Christian fortitude consists in strength of mind, through grace, exerted in two things. The first is ruling and suppressing the #emph[evil] and unruly passions and affections of the mind. The second is steadfastly and freely exerting and following #emph[good] affections and dispositions, without being hindered by sinful fear or the opposition of enemies. But the passions that are restrained and kept under in the exercise of this Christian strength and fortitude are the very passions that are vigorously and violently exerted in a false boldness for Christ. And the affections that are vigorously exerted in true fortitude are those Christian, holy affections that are directly contrary to them. Though Christian fortitude appears in withstanding and counteracting the enemies outside us, it appears much more in resisting and suppressing the enemies within us; for they are our worst and strongest enemies, and have the greatest advantage against us. The strength of the good soldier of Jesus Christ appears in nothing more than in steadfastly keeping the holy calm, meekness, sweetness and benevolence of his mind amid all the storms, injuries, strange behavior, and surprising acts and events of this evil and unreasonable world. The Scripture seems to suggest that true fortitude consists chiefly in this. Prov 16:32: “He who is slow to anger is better than a warrior, and he who controls his temper is greater than one who captures a city.”

The most direct and surest way in the world to judge rightly what a holy fortitude is, in fighting with God’s enemies, is to look to the captain of all God’s hosts, our great leader and example. We should see in what his fortitude and valor appeared in his chief conflict. That was the time of the greatest battle that ever was or ever will be fought with these enemies, when he fought with them all alone, and of the people there was no one with him. Then he exercised his fortitude in the highest degree he ever did, and won that glorious victory which will be celebrated in the praises and triumphs of all the hosts of heaven throughout all eternity. We should look, that is, to Jesus Christ at the time of his last sufferings, when his enemies on earth and in hell made their most violent attack on him, surrounding him on every side like tearing and roaring lions. Here, surely, we will see the fortitude of a holy warrior and champion in the cause of God in its highest perfection and greatest luster, an example fit for the soldiers who fight under this captain to follow. But how did he show his holy boldness and valor at that time? Not in the exercise of any fiery passions. Not in fierce and violent speeches, vehemently denouncing and crying out against the intolerable wickedness of his opponents, giving them what they deserved in plain terms. He showed it in not opening his mouth when he was afflicted and oppressed, in going like a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before its shearers is silent, not opening his mouth. He showed it in praying that the Father would forgive his cruel enemies, because they did not know what they were doing; in shedding not the blood of others, but his own, with all-conquering patience and love. Indeed, one of his disciples, who made an eager show of #emph[boldness for Christ] and confidently declared that he would sooner die with Christ than deny him, began to lay about him with a sword. But Christ meekly rebukes him, and heals the wound he gives. And never were the patience, meekness, love and forgiveness of Christ so gloriously displayed as at that time. Never did he appear so much a #emph[Lamb,] and never did he show so much of the #emph[dove-like] Spirit, as at that time. If, then, we see any of Christ’s followers, in the midst of the most violent, unreasonable and wicked opposition from God’s enemies and his own, keeping under all this temptation the humility, quietness and gentleness of a lamb, and the harmlessness, love and sweetness of a dove, we may well judge that here is a good soldier of Jesus Christ.

When persons are fierce and violent, and exert their sharp and bitter passions, it shows weakness instead of strength and fortitude. 1 Cor 3, at the beginning: “Brothers, I could not address you#super[+] as spiritual, but as carnal—as infants in Christ. … For you#super[+] are still carnal. For since there is jealousy and dissension among you#super[+], are you#super[+] not carnal? Are you#super[+] not walking in the way of man?”

There is a pretended boldness for Christ that arises from no better principle than pride. A man may be eager to expose himself to the dislike of the world, and even to provoke their displeasure, out of pride. For it is the nature of spiritual pride to make men seek distinction and singularity, and so often to set themselves at war with those they call carnal, so that they may be more highly exalted among their own party. True boldness for Christ is universal: it overcomes all, and carries men above the displeasure of friends and foes alike, so that they will forsake all rather than Christ, and will rather offend all parties and be thought little of by all than offend Christ. And the duty that tests whether a man is willing to be despised by those of his own party, and thought the least worthy of their regard, is a much more proper test of his boldness for Christ than his being eager to expose himself to the reproach of opponents. The apostle did not seek glory from heathens and #emph[Jews] only, nor from Christians either, as he declares in 1 Thess 2:6. #footnote[Mr. #emph[Shepard,] speaking of hypocrites who seek applause, says: “This is why men forsake their friends, and trample underfoot the scorn of the world: they have credit elsewhere. To keep their place in the love of godly men, they will suffer much.” Parable of the ten virgins, Part I. P. 180.] He is bold for Christ who has Christian fortitude enough to confess his fault openly, when he has committed one that requires it, and as it were to come down on his knees before his opponents. Things like these are far greater evidence of holy boldness than resolutely and fiercely confronting opponents.

As some are much mistaken about the nature of true #emph[boldness] for Christ, so they are about Christian #emph[zeal.] It is indeed a flame, but a sweet one; or rather, it is the heat and fervor of a sweet flame. For the flame of which it is the heat is no other than that of divine love, or Christian love, the sweetest and most benevolent thing that is or can be in the heart of man or angel. Zeal is the fervor of this flame as it goes out ardently and vigorously toward the good that is its object, in desiring it and pursuing it; and so, as a consequence, in opposing the evil that is contrary to it and hinders it. There is indeed opposition, and vigorous opposition, that is a part of it, or rather goes along with it. But it is against #emph[things,] not #emph[persons.] Bitterness against the #emph[persons] of men is no part of it, but is very contrary to it. So much is this so that the warmer true zeal is, and the higher it is raised, the further persons are from such bitterness, and the fuller of love, both to the evil and to the good. This appears from what has just been observed: in its very nature and essence it is nothing other than the fervor of a spirit of Christian love. And as for the opposition to #emph[things] that there is in it, it is first and chiefly against the #emph[evil things] in the person himself who has this zeal, against the enemies of God and holiness that are in his own heart (since these are most in his view, and what he has most to do with), and only secondarily against the sins of others. Therefore there is nothing in a true Christian zeal that is contrary to that spirit of meekness, gentleness and love, the spirit of a little child, a lamb and a dove, that has been spoken of. It entirely agrees with it, and tends to promote it.

But to say something in particular about this Christian spirit I have been speaking of, as it is exercised in these three things, #emph[forgiveness, love] and #emph[mercy,] I would observe that the Scripture is very clear and explicit about the absolute necessity of each of these, as belonging to the temper and character of every Christian.

It is so with a #emph[forgiving spirit,] a disposition to overlook and forgive injuries. Christ gives it to us as both a negative and a positive evidence. He teaches us explicitly that if we are of such a spirit, it is a sign that we ourselves are in a state of forgiveness and favor; and that if we are not of such a spirit, we are not forgiven by God. And he seems to take special care that we should take good notice of it and always keep it in mind. Matt 6:12, 14, 15: “And forgive us our debts, as we also have forgiven our debtors. … For if you#super[+] forgive men their trespasses, your#super[+] heavenly Father will also forgive you#super[+]. But if you#super[+] do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your#super[+] Father forgive yours.” Christ says the same again at another time (Mark 11:25, 26). And again in Matthew 18:22 to the end, in the parable of the servant who owed his lord ten thousand talents, who would not forgive his fellow servant a hundred denarii, and was therefore handed over to the torturers. In the application of the parable Christ says in verse 35: “That is how My heavenly Father will treat each of you#super[+] unless you#super[+] forgive your brother from your heart.”

And the Scripture is very plain and abundant that all true saints are of a #emph[loving,] benevolent and beneficent disposition. The apostle tells us that without it, though we “speak in the tongues of men and of angels,” we are “only a ringing gong or a clanging cymbal”; and though we “have the gift of prophecy and can fathom all mysteries and all knowledge,” yet without this spirit we are “nothing.” And there is no other virtue or disposition of the mind that is so often and so explicitly insisted on in the marks laid down in the New Testament for knowing true Christians. It is often given as a sign that is especially distinguishing, by which all may know Christ’s disciples, and by which they may know themselves; and it is often laid down as both a negative and a positive evidence. Christ calls the law of love, above all others, #emph[his commandment.] John 13:34: “A new commandment I give you#super[+]: Love one another. As I have loved you#super[+], so you#super[+] also must love one another.” And 15:12: “This is #emph[My commandment,] that you#super[+] love one another as I have loved you#super[+].” And verse 17: “This is My command to you#super[+]: Love one another.” And he says in 13:35: “By this everyone will know that you#super[+] are My disciples, if you#super[+] love one another.” And in 14:21 (still with special reference to this which he calls #emph[his] commandment): “Whoever has My commandments and keeps them is the one who loves Me.” The beloved disciple, who had so much of this sweet temper himself, dwells on it at length in his epistles. None of the apostles does so much as he does in laying down explicit signs of grace by which those who profess religion may test themselves. And in his signs he insists on scarcely anything else but a spirit of Christian love and a practice that agrees with it. 1 John 2:9, 10: “If anyone claims to be in the light but hates his brother, he is still in the darkness. Whoever loves his brother remains in the light, and there is no cause of stumbling in him.” 3:14: “We know that we have passed from death to life, because we love our brothers. The one who does not love remains in death.” Verses 18, 19: “Little children, let us love not in word and speech, but in action and truth. And by this we will know that we belong to the truth, and will assure our hearts in His presence.” Verses 23, 24: “This is His commandment: … that we should love one another. … Whoever keeps His commandments remains in God, and God in him. And by this we know that He remains in us: by the Spirit He has given us.” 4:7, 8: “Beloved, let us love one another, because love comes from God. Everyone who loves has been born of God and knows God. Whoever does not love does not know God, because God is love.” Verses 12, 13: “No one has ever seen God; but if we love one another, God remains in us, and His love is perfected in us. By this we know that we remain in Him, and He in us: He has given us of His Spirit.” Verse 16: “God is love; whoever abides in love abides in God, and God in him.” Verse 20: “If anyone says, ‘I love God,’ but hates his brother, he is a liar. For anyone who does not love his brother, whom he has seen, cannot love God, whom he has not seen.”

And the Scripture is as plain as it is possible for it to be that none are true saints but those whose true character it is to be of a disposition to #emph[pity] and #emph[relieve] their fellow creatures who are poor, needy and afflicted. Ps 37:21: “The righteous are gracious and giving.” Verse 26: “They are ever generous and quick to lend.” Ps 112:5: “It is well with the man who is generous and lends freely.” Verse 9: “He has scattered abroad his gifts to the poor.” Prov 14:31: “Whoever honors Him is kind to the needy.” Prov 21:26: “The righteous give without restraint.” Jer 22:16: “He took up the cause of the poor and needy, and so it went well with him. Is this not what it means to know Me?” declares the #smallcaps[Lord]. Jas 1:27: “Pure and undefiled religion before our God and Father is this: to care for orphans and widows in their distress,” and so on. Hos 6:6: “For I desire mercy, not sacrifice, and the knowledge of God rather than burnt offerings.” Matt 5:7: “Blessed are the merciful, for they will be shown mercy.” 2 Cor 8:8: “I am not making a demand, but I am testing the sincerity of your#super[+] love in comparison to the earnestness of others.” Jas 2:13, 14, 15, 16: “For judgment without mercy will be shown to anyone who has not been merciful. … What good is it, my brothers, if someone claims to have faith, but has no deeds? Can such faith save him? Suppose a brother or sister is without clothes and daily food. If one of you#super[+] tells him, ‘Go in peace; stay warm and well fed,’ but does not provide for his physical needs, what good is that?” 1 John 3:17: “If anyone with earthly possessions sees his brother in need, but withholds his compassion from him, how can the love of God abide in him?” In the description he gives us of the day of judgment in Matt 25 (the most detailed we have in the whole Bible), Christ represents that judgment will be passed on that day according to whether men have been found to be of a merciful spirit and practice or not. Christ’s design in giving such a description of the proceedings of that day is plainly to impress on all his followers the conviction that unless this was their spirit and practice, there was no hope of their being accepted and acknowledged by him on that day. This, therefore, is a conviction we ought to be possessed with. We find in Scripture that a #emph[righteous man] and a #emph[merciful man] are synonymous expressions. Isa 57:1: “The #emph[righteous] perish, and no one takes it to heart; #emph[merciful men] are swept away, while no one considers that the #emph[righteous] are taken away from the presence of evil.”

So we see how full, clear and abundant the evidence from Scripture is that those who are truly gracious are under the government of that lamb-like, dove-like Spirit of Jesus Christ, and that this is essentially and eminently the nature of the saving grace of the gospel, and the proper spirit of true Christianity. We may therefore determine without doubt that all truly Christian affections are attended with such a spirit, and that this is the natural tendency of the fear and hope, the sorrow and joy, the confidence and zeal of true Christians.

Let no one take me to mean that true Christians have no remains of a contrary spirit, and can never in any instance be guilty of behavior that does not agree with such a spirit. But this I affirm, and will affirm until I deny the Bible to be worth anything: that everything in Christians that belongs to true Christianity has this tendency and works this way. And there is no true Christian on earth who is not so under the prevailing power of such a spirit that he is properly named from it, and it is truly and justly his character. Therefore ministers and others have no warrant from Christ to encourage persons whose character and behavior are contrary to it to think they are converted because they tell a fair story of illuminations and manifestations. In doing so they would set up their own wisdom against Christ’s, and judge without, and against, the rule by which Christ has declared that all men should know his disciples. Some persons place religion so much in certain passing illuminations and impressions (especially if they come in a particular method and order), and so little in the spirit and temper persons are of, that they greatly deform religion, and form notions of Christianity quite different from what it is as portrayed in the Scriptures. The Scripture knows of no true Christians who are of a sordid, selfish, cross and contentious spirit. Nothing more absurd can be invented than a morose, hard, close-fisted, haughty, spiteful true Christian. We must learn the way of bringing men to rules, and not of bringing rules to men and so straining and stretching the rules of God’s word to take in ourselves and some of our neighbors, until we make them of no effect at all.

It is true that allowances must be made for men’s natural temperament with regard to these things, as with others. But not such allowances as to allow that men who were once wolves and serpents are now converted, without any remarkable change in the spirit of their mind. The change made by true conversion is usually most remarkable and perceptible with respect to the wickedness the person was most notoriously guilty of before. Grace has as great a tendency to restrain and mortify the sins that are contrary to the spirit that has been spoken of as it has to mortify drunkenness or lewdness. Indeed, the Scripture represents the change worked by gospel grace as appearing especially in an alteration of the first sort. Isa 11:6–9: “The wolf will live with the lamb, and the leopard will lie down with the goat; the calf and young lion and fatling will be together, and a little child will lead them. The cow will graze with the bear, their young will lie down together, and the lion will eat straw like the ox. The infant will play by the cobra’s den, and the toddler will reach into the viper’s nest. They will neither harm nor destroy on all My holy mountain, for the earth will be full of the knowledge of the #smallcaps[Lord] as the sea is full of water.” And Isa 65:25 is to the same purpose. Accordingly we find that in the early times of the Christian church converts were remarkably changed in this respect. Titus 3:3 and following: “For at one time we too were foolish, disobedient, misled, and enslaved to all sorts of desires and pleasures—living in malice and envy, being hated and hating one another. But when the kindness of God our Savior and His love for mankind appeared, He saved us … through the washing of new birth and renewal by the Holy Spirit.” And Col 3:7, 8: “When you#super[+] lived among them, you#super[+] also used to walk in these ways. But now you#super[+] also put aside all such things as these: anger, rage, malice, slander, and filthy language from your#super[+] lips.”
